`gl_index` is a module `var` list of the start offsets of each name in `GL_NAMES` (with a parallel list of lengths), built once on first use by splitting at spaces; the n-th name pairs with `GL_CPS[n]`.

`gl_hex(s const byte[:], out int[>..]&) bool` clears out and reads s as a run of groups of exactly 4 hex digits (upper or lower case), pushing each group's value; false when s is empty or its length is not a multiple of 4 or a byte is not a hex digit.

`gl_name_to_text(name const byte[:], out byte[>..]&) bool` appends the Unicode text of a glyph name to out (UTF-8 through `dm::dm_put_cp`) and returns whether it was recognised. Rules, in order: drop everything from the first period on (a name that becomes empty is not recognised); when the name contains underscores, it is a ligature: recognise each underscore-separated part with these same rules and append them all (not recognised when any part fails); "uni" followed by groups of 4 hex digits appends each group's code point (`gl_hex`); "u" followed by 4 to 6 hex digits appends that code point; a single ASCII letter appends itself; a name found in `GL_NAMES` appends its code point from `GL_CPS`; names made of "g", "glyph", "cid", "index" or "G" followed only by digits, and every other name, are not recognised.

`gl_std(code int) int` maps a StandardEncoding code to its code point: codes 32 to 126 map to themselves except 39 (0x2019) and 96 (0x2018); codes 160 to 255 map through `GL_STD_HI[code - 160]` (0 means undefined); everything else is 0.

`gl_base_width(font const byte[:], code int) real` returns the width in thousandths of an em of a base-14 font for a code in WinAnsi or Standard encoding: when the font name contains "Courier" the width is 600; codes 32 to 126 use `GL_TIMES` when the name contains "Times" or "Roman" or "Serif" or "Georgia" or "Garamond" or "Cambria" or "Book", and `GL_HELV` otherwise; other codes use 500 for Times-like names and 556 otherwise; when the name contains "Bold" multiply by 1.05.

```amni
import dm;
```
